The second probe lacks the phenolic OH, eliminating efficient phenoxyl-radical formation (B). The residual light-dependent Cy5 signal must therefore arise from a different reactive intermediate that still carries the alkyne. Both probes share the strained bicyclo[4.2.0]octadiene core, which can undergo sensitized retro-[2+2] cycloreversion under the same 417 nm/thioxanthone conditions to release the electrophilic Michael acceptor methyl (E)-4-oxo-4-(prop-2-yn-1-ylamino)but-2-enoate. This species alkylates protein nucleophiles, thereby installing the alkyne that is subsequently clicked.  

A is common to both experiments and does not itself become covalently attached to proteins via an alkyne. D is not generated from the given structures. E is added after irradiation and is identical in light/dark controls. Thus only C accounts for a light-dependent, probe-dependent labeling event that survives removal of the phenol.  

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

