The second probe lacks the phenolic OH (abolishing efficient phenoxyl-radical formation), but retains the identical strained bicyclo[4.2.0]octadiene core that bears the alkyne. Sensitized retro-[2+2] cleavage of that core therefore remains available (even if less efficient once the phenol is removed) and directly releases the electrophilic Michael acceptor methyl (E)-4-oxo-4-(prop-2-yn-1-ylamino)but-2-enoate. This species still carries the alkyne, alkylates protein nucleophiles (albeit far less efficiently than a phenoxyl radical), and thereby accounts for the weaker but still observable light-versus-dark fluorescence difference.

A is identical across all light/dark arms and lacks any alkyne; B/D are eliminated by the structural swap or by structure; E is added after irradiation. Thus the molecule responsible is C.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

